r a Big Blind checking option.\nAlternatively, the opponent might have a Queen (Top Pair) and was slow-playing or trapping, finally betting for value. Or they hit a pair of 6s with a better kicker, or a set of 6s.\nHowever, it is also possible they are bluffing because the board is scary (possible straights) or simply trying to steal a small pot.\nGiven the checks throughout, my Pair of 6s is actually a reasonable bluff-catcher, but the bet size is significant relative to the likely pot. \nIf the opponent has a 5, I lose. If the opponent has a Q, I lose. If the opponent has a pair of 4s, 3s, or 2s, I win (unless they have two pair).\nCalling is risky because the sudden aggression usually signals *something* changed or they are confident. A pair of 6s is 4th pair on the board (Q, 6, 4, 3, 2) effectively (actually 2nd pair in rank, but vulnerable). Wait, Q is high, then 6. So I have 2nd pair.\nDoes the opponent have a 5? Any 5 makes a straight. A random 5 is very possible in a limped/checked pot.\nDoes the opponent have a Q? Possible.\nDoes the opponent have nothing? Possible, but less likely to fire 656 into a tiny pot without equity.\n\nFold is the safest option here. The pot is small, I have plenty of chips left (19900), and a sudden river bet after checking three streets often indicates a made hand (straight or top pair) or a strong bluff that isn't worth catching with just 2nd pair weak kicker.\n\nReason:\nThe opponent checked three streets and woke up with a significant bet on the River. The River card ($6\\diamondsuit$) connects a straight for any hand holding a 5 (2-3-4-5-6). Since the opponent checked pre-flop and flop, holding a low card like a 5 is very plausible. Even if they don't have the straight, they could easily hold a Queen (Top Pair) played passively. While I have 2nd pair, my kicker is mediocre, and the risk of the straight or a better pair is too high to justify calling a large bet for a small pot. Preserving the stack is better.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
